diff --git a/docs/profile-image-active-storage.md b/docs/profile-image-active-storage.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..56a38db
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/profile-image-active-storage.md
@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+# Profile picture storage: first migration deploy
+
+Only **User** and **Organization** profile pictures now write to Active Storage
+(`profile_image_file`). Tags, tweets, organization cover images, and all other
+CarrierWave uploads are unchanged.
+
+## Deploying
+
+1. Back up the database and existing upload storage as usual. Run `bundle install`
+   (the S3 service requires `aws-sdk-s3`).
+2. Run `bin/rails db:migrate` before starting the new application processes. This
+   adds only Active Storage's tables; it does not scan, download, rewrite, or delete
+   existing profile pictures or alter their CarrierWave columns.
+3. Deploy the web and Sidekiq processes together. Active Storage analysis and purge
+   jobs use Sidekiq's existing `default` queue. Ensure `/rails/active_storage/*`
+   reaches the new Rails application, including through the CDN/reverse proxy.
+4. Verify a legacy avatar and a newly uploaded avatar can both be fetched publicly
+   through the UI/API and the configured image optimizer.
+
+Production storage follows the existing upload configuration:
+
+- `FILE_STORAGE_LOCATION=file`: disk under `storage/`.
+- `FOREM_CONTEXT=forem_cloud`: the existing `AWS_BUCKET_NAME`, `us-east-2`, and the
+  instance IAM role. The role must allow reading, writing, and deleting the new
+  Active Storage object keys, not just the old `uploads/` prefix.
+- Otherwise, with `AWS_ID`: the existing `AWS_ID`, `AWS_SECRET`, `AWS_BUCKET_NAME`,
+  and `AWS_UPLOAD_REGION` (falling back to `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`).
+- Otherwise: disk under `storage/`.
+
+Disk storage must be persistent and shared between application instances, just as
+production CarrierWave disk storage must be. Include `storage/` and the Active
+Storage tables in backups. Development uses `storage/`; tests use `tmp/storage/`.
+
+Active Storage images are delivered using permanent signed application URLs,
+which redirect to storage. Expiring S3 service URLs are not stored in article
+caches, notifications, or API responses. Legacy URLs keep their original
+CarrierWave directory, filename, asset host, and storage configuration.
+
+## Compatibility and later deploys
+
+Readers prefer an Active Storage attachment when one exists, otherwise they use
+CarrierWave. Upload forms, remote provider avatars, generated defaults, bots, and
+API organization uploads continue using their existing setters. Image size/type
+checks, image-bomb/frame protection, EXIF processing, and safe remote downloads
+still run through a temporary CarrierWave uploader, but its file is never stored
+permanently in CarrierWave. Active Storage analysis also uses the existing
+ImageMagick installation, rather than introducing a libvips runtime requirement.
+
+Blank upload fields leave the current picture alone. Invalid images, failed
+remote downloads, and model validation failures do not replace the stored picture.
+Replacing a legacy avatar leaves its old column/key and object intact, so existing
+URLs and cached content continue working. Attachment changes still invalidate the
+avatar caches and refresh organization article caches/social images.
+
+There is deliberately **no backfill or legacy cleanup in this deploy**. Retain the
+CarrierWave mount, columns, objects, and URL configuration until a separately
+verified backfill and cache-retention plan permits removing them. The table
+migration cannot be rolled back destructively after new pictures have been stored.
+Once new uploads are enabled, code rollbacks must retain this dual-read bridge and
+Active Storage routes: a CarrierWave-only release cannot read new attachments.
